{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ס\"ת", :translit "sa\"t", :gloss "the final letters"} {:he "צבור", :translit "tzibbur", :gloss "congregation"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "וייקץ יעקב משנתו", :spells "צבור"}], :frame {:text "The principle that prayer is heard specifically in a congregation appears in multiple Talmudic sources. Berakhot 8a states that a person's prayer is heard only together with the congregation. The term tzibbur is expounded as an acronym for tzadikim, beinonim, and resha'im in Shabbat 32a.", :sources ["Berakhot 8a" "Shabbat 32a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "משנתו", :translit "mishnato", :gloss "his sleep"} {:he "ממשנתו", :translit "mimishnato", :gloss "from his learning"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay linking 'sleep' (mishnato) with 'learning' (mishnato) by reading the consonants differently."}], :frame {:text "The Talmud establishes that Torah study should occupy a person day and night, as derived from Joshua 1:8 and expounded in Menahot 99b. The idea that Torah study accompanies a person even in sleep is expressed in the teaching that Torah songs linger on one's lips during sleep, cited from a verse in the Shema context in Berakhot 4b. The motif of a righteous person's heart engaged in Torah even while sleeping is found in the exegesis of Proverbs 6:22 and parallels in the Talmudic discussion of Torah as a perpetual companion.", :sources ["Menahot 99b" "Berakhot 4b"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "וייקץ יעקב משנתו", :spells "צבור", :letters ["ץ" "ב" "ו"], :by :selah, :order :mismatch} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay linking 'sleep' (mishnato) with 'learning' (mishnato) by reading the consonants differently.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:08:55.680014373Z", :model "glm-5.2"}